        <time datetime="2026-08-06" class="text-sm text-gray-400 not-prose">August 6, 2026</time>
        <span class="not-prose inline-block ml-3 px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-medium bg-blue-100 text-blue-800">Templates</span>
        <h1>6 Response Templates for the Google Reviews That Leave You Staring at a Blank Screen</h1>

        <p>The standard response advice covers two scenarios. A customer writes something positive - thank them, be specific, maybe mention you look forward to seeing them again. A customer writes a complaint - acknowledge the issue, apologize, offer to make it right offline. That framework handles the vast majority of what comes in.</p>

        <p>The rest is harder. A review shows up and something is off. The reviewer has the wrong business entirely. A glowing write-up quotes a price that changed months ago. A generous paragraph has a real complaint buried in its third sentence. The text is in a language you can't identify. Nobody gave you a script for any of this, so you type and delete a few sentences, give up, and move on without responding.</p>

        <p>That's the wrong call. Every review - including the strange ones - is visible to future customers reading your profile. A non-response to a misattributed review or a factually incorrect 5-star doesn't just fail to help - it leaves the record standing as written.</p>

        <p>These six templates are built for those situations. For each one: a brief description of why it's tricky, the template itself, and a short note on what the template is actually doing.</p>

        <h2>When the Reviewer Has Confused You With Another Business</h2>

        <p>This happens to businesses with common names, businesses operating near a similarly named competitor, or businesses that recently moved or rebranded. A review intended for someone else lands on your listing. The rating and the details describe an experience that didn't happen with you.</p>

        <p>The response is more delicate than it looks. You need to flag the mismatch without accusing the reviewer of anything, signal to future readers that the content may be misattributed, and leave a door open in case you've read the situation wrong.</p>

        <blockquote>
            <p><strong>Template:</strong></p>
            <p>Hi [Name], thank you for taking the time to leave a review. We've read through your experience carefully and we don't believe this matches anything in our records - the details you've described don't correspond to a visit or interaction on our end. If there's any chance this was intended for a different business, we hope you're able to find the right listing. And if we've misread things, please don't hesitate to reach us directly at [contact info] - we want to make sure every customer is heard.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The key phrase is "we don't believe this matches anything in our records." It expresses uncertainty rather than a flat accusation. Future readers see that the business owner noticed and looked into it. The reviewer isn't publicly called out as confused, which keeps the tone civil. If they did have the wrong listing, the path to correcting it is clear.</p>

        <p>If the misattributed review is negative, the temptation is to say more - to defend yourself against something that didn't happen. Keep the same measured tone regardless. Escalating in defense of a review that isn't even about you reads poorly no matter how justified it feels.</p>

        <h2>When Someone Reviews You Based on What They Heard, Not What They Experienced</h2>

        <p>This is different from a fake review. It's usually a friend or family member of someone who had a negative experience - someone who heard the story and decided to add their voice. Or it's someone who came across a post on social media, felt strongly, and found your listing. The reviewer was never your customer. They may have never interacted with your business in any way.</p>

        <p>Google's policies prohibit reviews from people who haven't had a genuine firsthand experience, and these can sometimes be flagged for removal through your Business Profile dashboard. That process can take time, though, and in the meantime a response is still necessary.</p>

        <blockquote>
            <p><strong>Template:</strong></p>
            <p>Thank you for sharing this. We want to be transparent: we can't locate any record of an interaction that matches what you've described, which suggests you may not have visited us directly. If someone you know had an experience with us that concerns you, we'd genuinely want to hear about it. Please reach out at [phone/email] - we take every concern seriously and want to understand what happened.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The tone is genuine curiosity rather than defensiveness. You're not calling the reviewer a liar - you're saying the pieces don't match what you know. This matters in two directions. Future readers see a business owner who is attentive and open to resolution. And occasionally, this kind of response prompts the original customer - the one with the actual experience - to contact you directly. That gives you a chance to resolve something real that might otherwise have stayed unresolved and unaddressed.</p>

        <h2>When a 5-Star Review Contains a Factual Error About Your Pricing, Hours, or Services</h2>

        <p>You receive a glowing, enthusiastic review. It's the kind you'd want at the top of your profile. And somewhere inside it, the reviewer quotes a price that's no longer accurate, describes hours you changed months ago, or praises a service you discontinued.</p>

        <p>The instinct is to leave it alone. They loved you, they gave you five stars - why complicate it? The problem is that future readers take that information at face value. When they show up at 6pm expecting you to be open until 8, or when they call expecting a price that stopped existing a year ago, the trust damage runs in two directions: toward the reviewer, who looks unreliable, and toward your business, which appears to have let a misleading review stand uncorrected.</p>

        <blockquote>
            <p><strong>Template:</strong></p>
            <p>Thank you so much for this kind review - we're really glad the experience hit the mark and we appreciate you sharing it! One small note for anyone reading: our [pricing / hours / service] has changed since your visit, and we'd hate for anyone to make plans based on information that's no longer current. The latest details are always up to date at [your website or Google listing]. We hope to see you again soon!</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Lead with genuine appreciation. Don't open with the correction - that reads as ungrateful and deflates the warmth of the original review. The phrase "since your visit" is deliberate: it implies the reviewer was accurate at the time, which is almost certainly true. You're not correcting them - you're updating the record. The response also gently drives future readers to your listing for current details, which is a small but useful redirect.</p>

        <h2>When the Review Is Written in a Language You Don't Speak</h2>

        <p>If your business serves a multilingual community, operates near a tourist destination, or simply has a listing with any international visibility, you will eventually receive a review in a language you can't read. The temptation is to skip it. You don't know what they said, and you can't respond to something you don't understand.</p>

        <p>Don't skip it. Non-response to this review signals to your multilingual customers - and to anyone who speaks that language and sees your profile - that you only pay attention to certain reviews. That impression is visible to everyone reading.</p>

        <blockquote>
            <p><strong>Template:</strong></p>
            <p>[Your response translated into the reviewer's language - see the note below]</p>
            <p>[Your primary language:] Thank you for your review! We hope you enjoyed your experience with us. If you have any questions or would like to share more, we'd love to hear from you.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Use a translation tool to produce a version of your response in the reviewer's language, and put that first. An imperfect machine translation is better than silence. Add your own-language version below for other readers. If the translated review is negative, respond to the substance of the complaint in both languages - don't use the language barrier as cover to avoid the content.</p>

        <p>If you genuinely can't identify the language, a short response in your primary language is still worth posting: "Thank you for your review. We'd love to hear more about your experience if you'd like to share." It's a gesture that anyone can read, in any language.</p>

        <h2>When a Glowing Review Has a Real Complaint Buried in the Middle</h2>

        <p>The review is four or five stars. The reviewer is clearly positive overall. But somewhere in the text - often sandwiched between two warm sentences - is a concrete complaint: a long wait, an issue with a staff interaction, a problem that needed more attention. The reviewer was generous enough not to let it affect their rating, but they mentioned it.</p>

        <p>Most businesses respond to the tone and skip the buried complaint entirely. That's a missed opportunity in two ways. Future readers who read carefully will notice the issue - and notice that you didn't. And the original reviewer included it because it mattered to them. Ignoring it confirms that the feedback didn't land.</p>

        <blockquote>
            <p><strong>Template:</strong></p>
            <p>Thank you so much for this - we're really glad the experience was positive overall! We also noticed your comment about [the specific issue they mentioned], and we want you to know that didn't get past us. We're looking into it and we appreciate you flagging it, even in the context of a great visit. That kind of detail is genuinely useful. We look forward to seeing you again.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The phrase "that didn't get past us" is doing real work. It signals careful reading and accountability. Future readers see a business that pays attention to the full text of a review, not just the stars. Address the complaint by name - "your note about the wait at checkout" rather than a vague "your feedback" - because specificity proves you actually read it.</p>

        <p>This kind of response also tends to land well with the original reviewer. Knowing that their aside was taken seriously increases the chance they return as a loyal customer rather than letting the unaddressed issue quietly compound into a different kind of review next time.</p>

        <h2>When the Review Names Your Business but Describes an Experience at a Different Location</h2>

        <p>For businesses with more than one location, this is a recurring issue. A customer had an experience at your downtown branch, but their Google app associated them with your suburban location and the review posted there instead. Or they searched for your brand name and clicked on whichever listing appeared first. The complaint is real. The location on the review is wrong.</p>

        <p>You can't dismiss it - the experience happened. You also can't respond as if it happened at the location where it posted, because that response won't reflect what the right team actually did or can do.</p>

        <blockquote>
            <p><strong>Template:</strong></p>
            <p>Thank you for reaching out, and we're sorry to hear this experience wasn't what you expected. Based on the details you've shared, it looks like this may have been meant for our [other location name or address] - but that doesn't change how seriously we take it. We'd love to connect directly and make sure the right people follow up with you. Please reach us at [contact info] and we'll take care of it from there.</p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Don't lead with the location error - that reads as defensive and shifts the focus away from the customer's experience. Acknowledge what happened first. The location clarification comes after, framed as useful context rather than a deflection. Close with a genuine offer to resolve the issue, which is both the right thing to do and the right visible signal for future readers checking that particular listing.</p>

        <p>If you connect with the reviewer and the issue gets resolved, there's a reasonable chance they'll update or remove the review on their own. That outcome isn't guaranteed, but it's far more likely if the conversation started well.</p>

        <h2>A Note on All Six</h2>

        <p>What these scenarios have in common is that none of them fit the standard positive-or-negative binary, and all of them tempt you toward either a generic response or no response at all. Both of those are worse than a response that acknowledges what's actually happening.</p>

        <p>Responding to unusual reviews is not about damage control. It's about demonstrating that a real person reads every review - the warm ones, the strange ones, and the ones that don't seem to have a clean answer. That pattern of attentiveness, visible across your profile over time, builds a kind of credibility that a high star average alone cannot.</p>

        <p>Keep these templates somewhere accessible. You won't need most of them every week. But when a review lands that leaves you uncertain how to respond, you'll be glad you didn't have to work it out from scratch.</p>

